ConoSorter - A Large-scale Cone Snail Transcriptome/Proteome Analysis Program
Version 1.0; October 2013
Copyright © 2013 Institute for Molecular Biosciences (IMB) - The University of Queensland.

HMMER - profile hidden Markov models for biological sequence analysis
http://hmmer.org/
Version 3.1b1; May 2013
Copyright © 2013 Howard Hughes Medical Institute.
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

ConoSorter is a high-throughput standalone program that implements regular expressions and profile Hidden
Markov Models (pHMMs) for large-scale identification and classification of precursor conopeptides into gene
superfamilies and classes based on the ER signal, pro-, and mature conopeptide regions generated from raw
next-generation transcriptomic or proteomic data.

ConoSorter also generates a set of relevant additional information (frequency of protein sequences, length,
number of cysteine residues, hydrophobicity rate of N-terminal region) and automatically searches ConoServer
database to allow the user to assess the reliability and relevance of the results and to aid the
identification of new conopeptide superfamilies and classes.

Content:
   GET STARTED
   USAGE
   RESULTS
   PROGRAM UPDATE
   LICENSE
   CITATION

--------------------------------------

GET STARTED
ConoSorter doesn't require any compilation. "ConoSorter" file, its appendix files ("transl", "regex_d", "regex-p",
"pHMM", "layout"), as well as the "models" folder should be placed in the same shell's search PATH directory.
The shebangs of "ConoSorter" and its appendix files might need to be changed accordingly. Access rights of
these later may also need to be changed to make them executable.
The latest version of gawk (available from the GNU project's at http://ftp.gnu.org/gnu/gawk/), as well as
HMMER package 3.1b1 (available at http://hmmer.janelia.org/) need to be installed in the same PATH directory.

Additional requirements for Windows operating systems:
In addition of the two programs mentioned previously, Windows users must install Cygwin in order to create a
Unix-like environment and command-line interface (more information at http://www.cygwin.com/).

Then, the user can check that the program has been installed properly by running the two test files provided
("test_dna.fasta" and "test_prot.fasta").

USAGE
Synopsis:
ConoSorter -[d|p|h] <input file>

* <input file> must be in fasta format.

Options:
* -d argument is mandatory and mentions that the input file contains cDNA sequences which will be
translated into amino acid sequences prior analysis.
* -p argument is mandatory and mentions that the input file contains amino acid sequences.
* -h option displays ConoSorter usage.

RESULTS
After processing the input file, ConoSorter will generate two separate files called "Regex.tab" and
"pHMM.tab", in which query sequences have been classified into conopeptide gene superfamilies and/or classes
by using regular expressions and pHMMs respectively. Additional information related to the sorted hits is
also reported. These two files could be imported in a spreadsheet program for reading convenience if needed.

PROGRAM UPDATE
* ConoServer database search function integrated in the "regex_d", "regex_p" and "pHMM" files can easily be
updated by adding new sequences at the end of the list. However the user has to respect the following syntax:
cono["sequence"]="name."
In case multiple names are given to a same conopeptide sequence, the formers have to be separated by
semi-colon(s):
cono["sequence"]="name1; name2."
* Regular expressions and pHMMs can be updated according to the methodology detailed in the referring
article (see CITATION).

